[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked *known: one issue, one fix queued*. The reports merged into it
say otherwise. Read by what each report describes instead of by subject, they are three problems with
three different fixes. The queued parser fix closes only the first one.

<CauseTickets ticket={4471} {tickets} />

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first.** Every tenant in the directory writes the
day first. A date like 7/4 comes out as 4 July instead of 7 April. This hits the app, email and the
`booking-v2` form. It is what #4471 says, and the queued fix is for this.

**`hint`: the `booking-v3` form tells people to type month first.** Its date field shows
`MM/DD/YYYY`, while the form reads the date the tenant's way, day first. So people do what the hint
says, and the booking lands a month off. Only tenants on `booking-v3` get this, and only through the
form. The parser fix does not touch it. The fix is the hint text, or the form reading what its hint asks for.

**`one-day`: bookings move by one day, not by a month.** These reports do not describe a day and
month swapped: "Tuesday 6 October showed as Monday", "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto
got Monday. Same meeting." The desk already read them this way (`desk-notes.json`), but `causes.ts`
files every report by its tenant, so they were counted under `parser`. A one-day shift between places
points at time zones (the invite's time converted in the wrong zone), not at the parser. The parser fix will not close it.

<Callout kind="warn">
Do not close #4471 when the parser fix ships. The <code>hint</code> reports stay open until the
<code>booking-v3</code> hint and the form agree, and the <code>one-day</code> reports until invites keep their day across time zones.
</Callout>

## How these were counted

- **By id, once.** The ticket export pages overlap, so some reports appear twice. Each report counts once (`uniqueById`).
- **By tenant, not by intake tag.** Intake tags a report from the reporter's browser at the moment it
  comes in, and that is a guess. The cause comes from the tenant's locale and booking form (`causes.ts`, `locales.ts`).
  The tags and the causes disagree in both groups. The [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) shows the tag counts.
- **The desk's way, as exported.** Every report counts under its tenant's cause. The 5 reports in `desk-notes.json` describe a one-day move, so they also have the `one-day` ticket, and they stay counted under `parser` too.
- **Not by subject.** [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone,
  and the problems share subjects ("Booking on the wrong date", "Date is wrong"). That is how they ended up in one ticket.
  It saw this coming: "engineering merges them later". This page is that split.

## What the desk can do next

- Triage new date reports from `booking-v3` form users to the `hint` ticket, and reports of a one-day move to the `one-day` ticket, not #4471.
- Consider a decision record that replaces 0001 for incidents: merge on cause (tenant, channel, form), not on subject. The
  [triage explorer](/kb/triage/dedupe) already shows pairs with the same subject and different causes.

